Q: Is it undefined behavior to assign arrays via punning struct members? A: <p>To avoid type punning and the UB it creates in C++, consider using <code>std::memcpy</code> as @Eljay has said.</p>
<p>When you optimize the build, in many compilers the <code>std::memcpy</code> is optimized away as they (the compiler) can (usually) see what you are trying to do.</p>
<p>This looks cleaner to me. Do this instead. I know exactly what is happening in this code snippet below without needing to untie the type punning knot.</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;cstring&gt;
#include &lt;iostream&gt;

int main() {
    int a[2] = {  1,  2 };
    int b[2] = { 10, 20 };
    static_assert(sizeof(b)==sizeof(a), &quot;b and a should be the same size&quot;);
    std::memcpy( a, b, sizeof(a) );
    std::cout &lt;&lt; a[0] &lt;&lt; &quot;, &quot; &lt;&lt; a[1] &lt;&lt; '\n';
}
</code></pre>
<p>Here is the <a href="https://godbolt.org/z/M9fnTqaPe" rel="nofollow noreferrer">compiler explorer (godbolt)</a>. Add -O2 to compiler flag and notice the memcpy goes away.</p>
<p>You can also add a static assert which will confirm the type of a and b are trivially copyable with <a href="https://en.cppreference.com/cpp/types/is_trivially_copyable" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>std::is_trivially_copyable</code></a>. Anytime you use a &quot;c style&quot; copy (ie: memcpy) you should make sure your type is trivially copyable as this is a fundamental difference between the C and the C++ programming language. Every type in C is trivially copyable but this is not the case in C++ where the copy constructor/assignment operator can be deleted and objects in C++ have a lifetime with a constructor/deconstructor and move semantics (which ends the lifetime of a variable before the deconstructor executes). For example: <code>std::mutex</code> is not copyable so you cannot use <code>std::memcpy</code> on mutexes.</p>
<p>In C programming language the union members have no notion of lifetime... in C++ union members have a lifetime. So you cannot use a union to type cast in C++. This is unrelated to the original question as no union is used but it is related in terms of type punning differences between C and C++; I am adding it to spread awareness.</p>

Q: Allow assert_* in test code, but otherwise forbid all constructs that can panic in production code A: <h2>Preventing panics the easy way (clippy)</h2>
<p>You can apply your clippy config (and many other configs) to all of your crates (Rust's word for packages) by using a <a href="https://doc.rust-lang.org/cargo/reference/workspaces.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">workspace</a>. In particular, you can put your workspace-wide clippy lints in your top-level workspace <code>Cargo.toml</code> under <code>[workspace.lints.clippy]</code>.</p>
<p>The blogpost <a href="https://emschwartz.me/your-clippy-config-should-be-stricter/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Your Clippy Config Should Be Stricter</a> will give you a sensible strict clippy config that you can apply to your workspace and catch the vast majority of panics that you are likely to write in your own code (as well as many other anti-patterns that aren't panic-related). The relevant snippet is this (edited to include <code>expect_used</code>):</p>
<pre class="lang-ini prettyprint-override"><code># Don't Panic - prevent panics from unwraps and unsafe slicing or indexing
string_slice = &quot;warn&quot;
indexing_slicing = &quot;warn&quot;
unwrap_used = &quot;warn&quot;
expect_used = &quot;warn&quot;
panic = &quot;warn&quot;
todo = &quot;warn&quot;
unimplemented = &quot;warn&quot;
unreachable = &quot;warn&quot;
get_unwrap = &quot;warn&quot;
unwrap_in_result = &quot;warn&quot;
unchecked_time_subtraction = &quot;warn&quot;
panic_in_result_fn = &quot;warn&quot;
</code></pre>
<p>The <code>arithmetic_side_effects</code> lint might also be helpful for you, but abiding by it is often very annoying.</p>
<p>Clippy doesn't have a dedicated lint to forbid assertions, but you can use the <code>disallowed_macros</code> lint with manual configuation in <code>clippy.toml</code>:</p>
<pre class="lang-ini prettyprint-override"><code>disallowed-macros = [
    &quot;std::assert&quot;,
    &quot;std::assert_eq&quot;,
    # etc
]
</code></pre>
<p>With that said, you <strong>should</strong> absolutely litter your code with <a href="https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/macro.debug_assert.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>debug assertions</code></a> everywhere you can think of an invariant that must not fail. Its paradoxical because debug assertions are meant to panic, but they are actually your best friend when it comes to preventing panics in production.</p>
<p>Clippy doesn't run on tests at all by default. You can run it on tests with <code>cargo clippy --all-targets</code>, but since you specifically don't want to apply these lints to tests I don't recommend it. If you do still want to lint your tests, you can allow things like unwraps and array indexing in tests by putting this in your workspace <code>clippy.toml</code> (this can't go in <code>Cargo.toml</code>):</p>
<pre class="lang-ini prettyprint-override"><code>allow-indexing-slicing-in-tests = true
allow-panic-in-tests = true
allow-unwrap-in-tests = true
allow-expect-in-tests = true
</code></pre>
<p>There is no option to disable <code>disallowed-macros</code> in tests though, so if you forbid <code>assert_eq!</code> in non-test code, you'll have to reenable it by putting this at the top of the <code>lib.rs</code> or <code>main.rs</code> in all of your crates:</p>
<pre class="lang-rust prettyprint-override"><code>#![cfg_attr(test, allow(clippy::disallowed_macros))]
</code></pre>
<h2>Preventing panics the hard way (linker shenanigans)</h2>
<p>If its absolutely critical, non-negotiable that your code must never panic under any circumstances, clippy is a great start but won't be enough, because you can't apply your clippy config to the standard library or any third-party dependencies. And if you found a way, I expect you'd get so many warnings that you have to go <code>no_std</code> and no dependencies, so linting &quot;the entire codebase&quot; would be a moot point.</p>
<p>However, if you absolutely must prevent panics in your dependencies, you can try a crate like dtolnay's <a href="https://docs.rs/no-panic/latest/no_panic/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">no_panic</a>. It forces the compiler to prove at link time that the annotated function can't panic. If it can't prove that it doesn't panic (either because it does panic or because the compiler isn't smart enough to see that it doesn't), then it will give a linker error. This does give a kind of inspection into third-party dependencies and the stdlib. However, this might be so difficult to satisfy that you still have to go <code>no_std</code> and no dependencies. Many (many) things can panic on edge cases that aren't checked by clippy. <code>println!</code> can panic if it can't write to <code>stdout</code> properly. <code>Vec::insert</code> can panic if the index is out of range. Sometimes these edge cases can be proven impossible with compiler optimizations, sometimes they can't.</p>
<p>Lastly, if you're ever tempted to &quot;fix&quot; code that might panic by replacing it with unsafe code (<code>unwrap_unchecked</code>, <code>assert_unchecked</code>, <code>unreachable_unchecked</code>, etc), don't. Panicking is always better than Undefined Behavior, even in production.</p>

Q: how to parse certificates using bouncy castle A: <pre><code>package com.incert.certmanager.scheduler;

import com.incert.certmanager.domain.Certificate;
import com.incert.certmanager.notification.NotificationService;
import com.incert.certmanager.repository.CertificateRepository;
import lombok.RequiredArgsConstructor;
import lombok.extern.slf4j.Slf4j;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Value;
import org.springframework.scheduling.annotation.Scheduled;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

import java.time.LocalDateTime;
import java.util.List;

@Slf4j
@Component
@RequiredArgsConstructor
public class CertificateExpiryScheduler {

    private final CertificateRepository certificateRepository;
    private final NotificationService notificationService;

    @Value(&quot;${app.scheduler.expiry-threshold-hours:72}&quot;)
    private int expiryThresholdHours;


    @Scheduled(cron = &quot;${app.scheduler.cron:0 0 * * * *}&quot;)
    public void checkExpiringCertificates() {
        int thresholdHours = expiryThresholdHours;
        LocalDateTime threshold = LocalDateTime.now().plusHours(thresholdHours);
        List&lt;Certificate&gt; expiring = certificateRepository.findAllExpiringBefore(threshold);

        if (expiring.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }
        notificationService.sendBatchExpiryAlert(expiring);
    }
}
</code></pre>
